    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically the seller just hands over the paperwork and the risk is on us once it's on the water. what kind of documentation do they actually need to provide us so we can make a successful claim if the container is breached? like what specific proof do we need to show the insurance company?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under CIF, the seller should provide the buyer with an original original or one copy of the Institute Cargo Clauses laid down (A, B, or C), which defines the extent of the insurance coverage. Additionally, the seller ought to give the buyer a copy of the insurance policy from the insurer, including a note or certificate of insurance showing the policy number, date, and coverage particulars. A detailed bill of lading or airbill, proving delivery of the goods to the carrier, is also essential. Lastly, the seller should ensure that all necessary notifications specifying your interests in the goods have been made to the insurer and to the transportation company. Such documentation will be vital for the buyer to prove the existence of an insurance policy and the terms of coverage when making an insurance claim for damaged goods.",
